  "rationale": "D3, data-informed. D2 with one law revised: the cool-flame burning rate no longer depends on oxygen, CO2 or pressure up to 1 atm (0.325 mm2/s at 2 mm, size exponent 0.15, helium +0.2 per 20 %, +0.5 per e-fold of pressure above 1 atm). Mechanism: a cool flame burns at a fixed flame temperature, so its rate follows the gas conductivity, not the oxygen. Train: twelve sustained cool flames in nine sessions burn at 0.31-0.36 mm2/s with no trend beyond 0.02 in oxygen (15-24 %), CO2 (0-25 %) or pressure (0.7-1 atm); D2 gave 0.41-0.44 where 0.34-0.36 was measured and 0.43 for a large droplet in air. The five statements are the cool-rate block of flex_heptane.wsp5 P0, typed on 14 November, copied unchanged. Not changed for want of train evidence: the radiative clock, the critical diameter, the widths, the point forecast (median). The validation run FLEX-035 was studied in detail by this lane before this patch was written; it is recorded as studied, not used to set numbers. Every number typed by the author; no fit, no search.",
  "origin": "system"
}
